  "narrative_body": "Israel's interception of a Gaza-aid flotilla in international waters — and the video an Israeli minister posted of blindfolded activists kneeling — gave Italy the occasion to activate EU diplomatic machinery against a specific Israeli official, the most concrete European foreign policy initiative Rome has mounted in this analysis period.\n\nAntonio Tajani, the foreign minister, formally requested that Kaja Kallas, the EU foreign policy chief, bring sanctions against Israeli Security Minister Ben-Gvir to the Foreign Affairs Council. Kallas accepted; the item is now on the June 15 agenda. France reinforced Rome's position by banning Ben-Gvir from its territory. The move was not reactive protest: Italy identified the instrument, used the institutional channel, and secured allied reinforcement. Sergio Mattarella, the head of state, Giorgia Meloni, the prime minister, Mr Tajani, and Guido Crosetto, the defence minister, all condemned the treatment of the 430 detained activists — 29 of them Italian — in notably unified terms. Matteo Salvini's response was milder (\"he shouldn't have done it\"), but the divergence was a footnote: the same pattern of managed coalition distance has appeared across several incidents without breaking the government's line.\n\nThe flotilla's reach extended further than the Mediterranean. A convoy component spent seven days at the gates of Sirte attempting to deliver aid to Gaza through eastern Libya before Haftar's forces turned it away — refusing to accept responsibility for the aid and sending the convoy back. Italy manages a bilateral relationship with Haftar's eastern Libyan authorities as part of its multi-faction Libya strategy. Italian citizens were part of a convoy those same authorities rejected. Rome has not commented on this dimension.\n\nEven as the flotilla dominated coverage, Ms Meloni received Narendra Modi for a bilateral summit at Villa Doria Pamphilj. Open-source reporting was thin — crowded out — but a government source confirms agreements were produced, though their content is not yet public. India is a BRICS member, a Quad participant, and the world's most populous democracy; a summit with Mr Modi is the strongest demonstration yet that Italy's positioning as a Western power with Global South reach is more than rhetoric. In the same week, Mr Tajani reaffirmed Italy's Hormuz role at the Festival dell'Economia di Trento. All three tracks of Italy's multi-vector strategy — EU diplomatic agency, Global South bridge-building, Mediterranean activism — were operational simultaneously for the first time in this analysis period. Washington was conspicuously absent: despite Italy and the United States sharing concern about Ben-Gvir's conduct, no US-Italy bilateral contact on Hormuz, the Sigonella refusal, or the flotilla was reported this week.\n\nThe structural question hanging over all of it is money. Christine Lagarde, the ECB president, appeared on Italian television on May 24 to publicly reject Ms Meloni's request to extend the national escape clause to energy spending: Rome must \"respect fiscal rules.\" Ms Meloni replied that \"extraordinary circumstances require EU responses.\" The exchange completed the request-rejection cycle she began with her May 17 letter to the European Commission. The stakes are immediate: Italy's €14.9 billion plan under the EU's SAFE defence-financing instrument must receive Treasury authorisation by May 30. Ms Meloni had conditioned Italy's participation on receiving the energy flexibility she has now been publicly denied. The government extended fuel excise cuts through early June and handed €200 million to road freight operators to head off a truckers' strike, showing it can absorb domestic pressure; the SAFE decision is a different order of problem. The competing interpretation — that Ms Meloni's letter was primarily coalition management, that both sides anticipated the rejection, and that SAFE will be approved on its original terms before the deadline — is gaining weight from how fast and how publicly the EU moved to refuse it.\n\nDomestically, the week produced the kind of controversy that confirms rather than creates a fault line. Chiara Colosimo, the FdI president of the Parliamentary Antimafia Commission, was photographed at a Capaci anniversary event smiling beside a Mussolini bust and arm-in-arm with Luigi Ciavardini, convicted in the 1980 Bologna station bombing that killed 85 people. Activists in Palermo named Ciavardini on protest banners during the commemoration. FdI officials defended Ms Colosimo. Giuseppe Conte, the Five Star leader, used the occasion for political attack. Mr Mattarella delivered a strong anti-mafia statement; Ms Meloni attended a separate commemoration. The government's response — neither condemning the associations nor pressing Ms Colosimo to step back — is consistent with its established approach when FdI's genealogical connections to neo-fascist networks surface at symbolic moments. Meanwhile, more than 6.6 million Italians were voting across 749 municipalities on May 24-25, with Venice the most watched race: the centre-right running a unified FdI-Lega-Forza Italia candidate against a centre-left alliance — the most direct test yet of whether the Schlein-Conte opposition coalition can win in a major city. Results were not available before this analysis closed.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
