      "summary": "Israel intercepted the Global Sumud Flotilla in international waters on May 20, detaining 430 activists including 29 Italians. Video posted by Israeli Security Minister Ben-Gvir showing activists blindfolded and kneeling provoked unified condemnation from Mattarella, Meloni, Tajani, and Crosetto; Italy formally requested EU sanctions against Ben-Gvir through Kallas, who accepted the request for discussion at the June 15 Foreign Affairs Council. France banned Ben-Gvir from its territory; Salvini's initially muted response ('he shouldn't have done it') diverged from coalition partners."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Italy's bid for EU Stability Pact flexibility on energy spending rebuffed by Lagarde; government extends fuel excise cuts, averts truckers' strike",
      "summary": "Meloni's formal request to the European Commission to extend the national escape clause — used for defense spending — to cover energy costs was publicly rejected by ECB President Lagarde on Italian television on May 24, who told Rome to respect fiscal rules; the government also passed a Council of Ministers decree extending fuel excise tax cuts through early June and providing €200M to truckers, defusing a threatened transport strike. Internal coalition tension between Crosetto (pushing for EU SAFE instrument funds for defense) and Economy Minister Giorgetti (urging caution on debt) featured prominently, and Reuters reported Italy faces a €50 billion investment gap to meet EU renewable energy targets."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Two Parma teachers attacked by student gang; Defense Minister Crosetto demands harsh consequences",
      "summary": "A group of students assaulted two teachers near the Parco della Musica in Parma on May 22, with video of the attack spreading on social media; Defense Minister Crosetto called for zero tolerance and 'severe consequences for all involved,' triggering a public row with local school authorities who argued schools should educate rather than punish. The incident sparked nationwide political debate on youth violence and school safety."
    }
  ]
}

